course: language
lesson: 44
title: Starving, a million times and a ton: everyday hyperbole
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Hyperbole (say it hy-PER-bo-lee) means making something sound much bigger, longer or heavier than it really is, on purpose, to show a strong feeling.
explain: When Leo bursts in after school shouting «Mum I'm STARVING, I haven't eaten in like a hundred years», nobody counts a hundred years: he had lunch at twelve, and he simply means «I'm really hungry».
explain: The trick is to split what is said into two parts: the feeling, which is real, and the number or size, which is made up. Mum's «I've told you a MILLION times» has a made-up number, but she really is cross.
explain: When you need the true size, look for the evidence: the clock, the scales, the worksheet, the phone. The clock beats the feeling.
fact: Hyperbole is exaggeration on purpose: saying something is far bigger, longer, heavier or worse than it is, to show how you feel.
fact: «I'm starving» three and a half hours after a full school lunch means «I'm quite hungry»; a piece of fruit until tea is the right-sized answer.
fact: In «I've told you a million times» the number is made up (maybe ten or twenty times), but the annoyance is real, so the towel goes on the hook.
fact: A ton is about a thousand kilograms, as heavy as a small car; a heavy school bag is perhaps five kilograms, so «it weighs a ton» means «it's too heavy».
fact: In the North East of England «pet» is a friendly word for a child, and «flippin' 'eck» is a mild way of showing surprise.
fact: «Forever», «a thousand years» and «a bajillion hours» are not times you can measure; «bajillion» and «gazillion» are made-up words for «loads», not real numbers.
fact: «Literally» is often used just to make words stronger, as in «I was literally flying»; people have used it like this for a long time, and it does not make the words true.
fact: «No cap» is young American slang for «no lie», but saying it does not turn an exaggeration into a fact.
fact: «My back's killing me» means it aches from work; pain that is very bad, sudden or does not go away is different, and the grown-up should see a doctor.
fact: In Scotland «chuckin' it doon» means raining very hard, «oot» means out, and «soaked tae the bone» means very wet, not hurt.
fact: «A mountain of clothes» is a big messy pile; the size word is the telling-off, so arguing «it's only knee-high» misses the point.
fact: When you need a real number, count or check: fifteen sums at about two minutes each take about half an hour, and a nan's «gazillion texts» can be counted on the phone.
exercise: Leo had lunch at twelve and at half past three shouts «I'm STARVING». What does he really mean? | He is quite hungry and would like a snack | He has not eaten for days and needs a doctor | He wants a big three-course dinner right now | He is cross that Mum forgot his lunch today
exercise: Mum says «I've told you a MILLION times, towel on the hook!» How many times has she really told Jay? | Many times, maybe ten or twenty | Exactly one million times, she counted | Only once, so it doesn't matter | Never, she has made it all up
exercise: In Mum's towel line, which part is real? | Her annoyance | The million | The count of towels | Nothing at all in it
exercise: Ava's gran says the school bag «weighs a ton, man». How heavy is it really? | Heavy for a child, perhaps five kilos | About a thousand kilos, like a car | As heavy as a pile of real bricks | Light, and Gran is only teasing her
exercise: What does «pet» mean when a gran from Newcastle says it to a child? | A friendly word for a child | A dog or a cat in the house | A rude name for someone small | A bag for carrying school books
exercise: Noah says «we've been here FOREVER bruh» at the dentist. How long have they really waited? | Twenty-five minutes | About an hour and a half | Since early yesterday | A whole school term
exercise: On the drive to the coast Dad says «It's been forty minutes, mate». Which time is real? | Dad's forty minutes | The bajillion hours | Both, they mean the same | Neither of the two times
exercise: What is a «bajillion»? | A made-up word for «loads» | A number bigger than a billion | A very long American car trip | An old word for a thousand
exercise: Zoe came second and says «I was literally flying, no lie». What is true? | She ran very fast and is proud of it | Her feet left the ground for a moment | She really came first in the race | She is lying to trick her friends
exercise: Dad has mowed all afternoon: «Oof, my back's killing me». What does he mean? | His back aches from the work | He is in danger and needs an ambulance | He cut it on the mower | He wants to keep mowing all evening
exercise: When should a grown-up's back pain be taken seriously? | When it is very bad, sudden or won't go away | Never, because grown-ups always exaggerate | Only when they use the word «killing» | Only after they have mowed a big lawn
exercise: Callum says it's «chuckin' it doon oot there». What is the weather doing? | Pouring with rain | Snowing very heavily | Hailing big stones | Windy but dry
exercise: Callum is «soaked tae the bone». What does he need? | A towel, dry clothes and a warm drink | A doctor to look at his wet bones | An umbrella so he can go back out | A plaster for where the rain hit him
exercise: Mason has fifteen sums and says they'll take «a thousand years, no cap». At about two minutes a sum, how long will they take? | About half an hour | About a thousand years | About two minutes | About fifteen hours
exercise: What does «no cap» mean? | No lie | No hats allowed in class | No homework tonight | No help from anyone
exercise: Mum says there's «a MOUNTAIN of clothes» on Ruby's floor. What does she want? | The clothes tidied up | Someone to measure how high the pile is | A photo of the mountain for her friends | New clothes, because these are dirty
exercise: Kofi's nan sent him «a gazillion texts». How can he find the real number? | Look at her phone and count them | Write «a gazillion» as the answer | Multiply a million by a thousand | Ask a friend to make a good guess
test: «bruh my mate says if u say literally it means its really true, so zoe actually flew??» What is right? | No, «literally» here just makes it stronger | Yes, «literally» always means it really happened | Yes, but only on sports day | No, Zoe made up the whole race
test: «Mum's told me a million times, so it's a made-up number, so I can ignore her, right?» | No, the number is made up but she really is cross | Yes, a made-up number means she doesn't mean any of it at all | Yes, mums only mean the things they can count | No, she has really said it a million times
test: What would happen if Ava tried to lift a real ton? | She couldn't: a ton weighs as much as a small car | She could lift it easily, it's about five kilos | She could lift it if Gran helped her a bit | It would feel lighter than her school bag
test: A cartoon shows Leo shrinking to a skeleton because he missed one snack. What is wrong with it? | Missing a snack makes you hungry, not a skeleton | Nothing, that is what starving after lunch means | He should have grown into a mountain instead | Nothing, because cartoons are always true
test: «are we there yet it's been a bajillion hours» — «It's been forty minutes, mate.» Your little sister asks how long you've driven. What do you tell her? | Forty minutes | A bajillion hours | Forever and ever | Nobody can know
test: «ugh the homework's gonna take a thousand years, might as well not start» What is the wise move? | Count the sums and start: it's about half an hour | Agree, nobody can do a thousand years of work | Do one sum a year until they are finished | Tell the teacher the homework is impossible
test: Dad sits down slowly after mowing: «Oof, my back's killing me». What is a kind answer? | Offer him a cuppa and a sit-down | Ring 999 for an ambulance | Tell him to go and mow next door | Say it's rude to talk about killing
test: ★★ Callum's «chuckin' it doon», Mason's «a thousand years, no cap» and Kofi's «a gazillion texts»: what do all three have in common? | Each makes something sound bigger to show a feeling | Each is a real measurement that you can trust and write down | Each is a Scottish word that you must learn | Each is a lie told to get someone into trouble
test: «Mum said there's a mountain in my room, I said it's only knee-high actually and she got madder lol why» | She meant «tidy it», so arguing about the size missed the point | She really believed there was a mountain in the room | Knee-high is taller than most mountains, so she was right | Ruby measured the wrong pile of clothes
test: ★★★ Leo had lunch at 12:00 and at 3:30 shouts «I haven't eaten in like a hundred years». How long has it really been since he ate? | Three and a half hours | Four and a half hours | Two and a half hours | A hundred years, just as he says
test: ★★★ Kofi says «my nan sent me a gazillion texts lol». She sent 3 in the morning, 4 at lunch and twice as many as at lunch in the afternoon. How many texts in all? | 15 | 11 | 14 | A gazillion
test: ★★★ Mum says the bag «weighs a ton», Ava says «it's like a hundred bricks», and the bathroom scales say 5 kg. What does the bag really weigh? | 5 kg | 1000 kg | 100 bricks | More than Mum
exam: Identify the figure of speech employed when a child, having eaten luncheon three and a half hours previously, declares that he is «starving». | Hyperbole, a deliberate exaggeration expressing a strong feeling | A literal statement of a condition requiring medical attention | Understatement, a deliberate diminution of the facts | A precise report of the hours elapsed since his meal
exam: It is stated by a parent that an instruction has been given «a million times». Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The number is not to be counted, though the displeasure is genuine | The instruction has in fact been issued on one million occasions | The statement is false and may accordingly be disregarded | The parent is engaged in an exercise of arithmetic
exam: Given that a ton is approximately one thousand kilograms, ascertain the probable mass of a school bag of which it is asserted that it «weighs a ton». | Several kilograms | One thousand kilograms precisely | The mass of a motor-car | No mass whatsoever
exam: Pursuant to a statement that fifteen sums «will take a thousand years», and given that each sum requires two minutes, determine the duration reasonably to be expected. | Approximately thirty minutes | Approximately one thousand years | Approximately two minutes | Approximately fifteen hours
